# =====================================================================
# Release V163 -- usp_Sync_Input_From_AppDB: Security = access-holders only
# =====================================================================
# Brings this proc in line with usp_Sync_Access_From_AppDB, which adopted the rule on 28/07 in its
# *02 and was never mirrored here. Adds the REVOKE delete and the access-holders filter on INSERT.
# The two procs' Security logic is now statement-for-statement identical.
#
# THE BUG: this proc inserted the WHOLE roster into Security.Application_Users, so every nightly
# build inflated it -- prod 7 -> 66, dev 19 -> 98 -- and the next access sync trimmed it back. The
# two procs have been fighting each other since July.
#
# Harmless, which is why nobody noticed: an access-less Security row fails closed in
# _get_user_info (there is a test for exactly that), so no one ever gained access they should not
# have. But for up to 10 minutes after every build, Security held dozens of rows with no business
# being there -- and Security is the auth surface, so it should mean what it says.
#
# REVOKE FIRST, for the same reason the access proc does: a user downgraded to no_access must LOSE
# the row, not keep an all-zero one. Only rows that JOIN to a staging row are touched, so support
# logins inserted straight into Security by SQL -- grace@ and andy@ on dev, which have no AppDB
# counterpart -- are left alone. That exclusion is load-bearing: sweeping them would lock support
# out of a tenant it may still need to act on.
#
# EXPECTED AFTER DEPLOY: running the nightly no longer changes Security.Application_Users at all.
# dev stays at its access-holder count, prod stays at 7. Before this, the count would spike.
#
# Run: .\Scripts\Deploy.ps1 -Manifest Releases\V163__input_sync_security_access_holders_only.manifest
# =====================================================================

DEPLOY   Fabric\Meta.usp_Sync_Input_From_AppDB.StoredProcedure.sql

TEST
